# Chapter 10 · NGO-isation of the Women's Movement

> The 2026 question asked candidates to "critically examine the '**NGO-ization**' of the women's movement in Pakistan. Has the donor-driven agenda of gender mainstreaming **depoliticized** the feminist struggle, converting it into a technocratic 'project' rather than a social revolution? Use the **WAD (Women and Development)** critique." This chapter teaches every word of that question.

## 10.1  The words

::: define NGO-isation | also spelt NGO-ization
The process by which a social movement's energy moves into **professional, formally registered, donor-funded non-governmental organisations**. Activists become paid staff; campaigns become **projects** with budgets, targets and reports; accountability runs **upwards to donors** rather than **outwards to members**.
-> The term was developed by scholars such as **Sonia Alvarez** (on Latin America, ==1999==) and **Islah Jad** (on the Arab world, ==2004==).
:::

::: define Depoliticisation | the central charge
Turning a struggle about **power** — who rules, who owns, who decides — into a **technical** problem to be managed by experts, training workshops and indicators. The question calls this a "**technocratic 'project'**" instead of a "**social revolution**".
:::

::: define WAD — Women and Development | the lens the question asks for
A 1970s critique, drawing on **Marxist and dependency theory**, of the earlier **WID** (Women in Development) approach. WID said women were "left out" of development and should be **integrated** into it. WAD replied that women have **always** been part of development — but on **unequal terms**, exploited by global capitalism and class structures. Adding women to projects does not change the system that exploits them.
-> Topic 4 teaches WID, WAD and GAD in full. Here, WAD is the tool for judging NGOs.
:::

## 10.2  How NGO-isation happened in Pakistan

::: fig timeline | The rise of women's NGOs in Pakistan.
@layout vertical
1975: Shirkat Gah — research and legal rights
1981: WAF — a voluntary, non-funded platform
1983: Applied Socio-economic Research (ASR) resource centre, Lahore
1985–86: Simorgh and Aurat Foundation founded
1990s: Beijing process, donor funding for "gender and development"; hundreds of NGOs
2000s: Gender Reform Action Plans; donor projects on political participation and violence
2010s: Tighter state regulation of foreign-funded NGOs (from 2015)
2018: Aurat March — self-funded, volunteer collectives
:::

- **Why it happened:** military rule closed political space in the 1980s; donors after Beijing (==1995==) funded "gender" projects; NGOs offered jobs, resources and some protection; the state welcomed service delivery.
- **What NGOs did well:** research on women's lives; legal aid and shelters; training of women councillors after ==2001==; drafting and lobbying for laws (harassment ==2010==, acid ==2011==, honour ==2016==); data on violence.

## 10.3  The case that NGO-isation depoliticised the movement

1. **Donor-driven agendas.** Priorities follow funding cycles and donor fashions (micro-credit, then governance, then violence) rather than members' needs.
2. **Projectisation.** Three-year projects with log-frames and "deliverables" cannot sustain long struggles against feudal, patriarchal or state power.
3. **Professionalisation.** Paid experts replace volunteers; activism becomes a career; English-language reports replace mass mobilisation.
4. **Upward accountability.** NGOs answer to donors, not to the women they serve.
5. **Class and urban bias.** Offices in Islamabad, Lahore and Karachi; rural and working-class women become "beneficiaries", not leaders.
6. **Legitimacy cost.** Foreign funding lets critics call feminism a "**Western agenda**".
7. **Competition and fragmentation.** NGOs compete for grants instead of building one movement.
8. **The WAD critique.** Projects that add women to income-generation or training leave untouched the **class structures and global economy** that exploit women's labour — they integrate women into an unequal system rather than change it.

::: fig cycle | The NGO-isation cycle, as its critics describe it.
@centre A movement becomes a project
Donor priorities: themes set abroad
Funding cycles: three-year projects
Professional staff: activists become employees
Upward reports: accountability to donors
Beneficiaries: women served, not organised
Less mobilisation: the street empties
:::

## 10.4  The case against (or for a balanced view)

1. **NGOs won laws.** Much of Pakistan's pro-women legislation since ==2000== was researched, drafted and lobbied by NGOs and women legislators working together.
2. **They kept space open** under military rule and in areas where political parties ignored women.
3. **Services save lives** — shelters, legal aid and helplines are not "depoliticised" to the woman who uses them.
4. **Not all NGOs are alike.** WAF refused to become a funded NGO; many groups combine services with advocacy.
5. **Movements revive.** The self-funded **Aurat March** (==2018==) shows that NGO-isation did not end grassroots feminism.
6. **Local funding and ownership** are growing, and state regulation since ==2015== has reduced dependence on foreign donors.

::: fig compare | A social movement and an NGO project.
## Social movement
- Goal: change power relations
- Members; volunteers
- Accountable to members
- Long-term, open-ended
- Protest, organising, politics
## NGO project
- Goal: measurable outputs
- Paid, professional staff
- Accountable to donors
- Short-term, fixed budget
- Training, services, advocacy
:::

## 10.4a  WID, WAD and GAD — the three lenses

::: table | Three approaches to women and development (full detail in Topic 4).
@widths 2,4,4,4
Aspect | WID (1970s) | WAD (late 1970s) | GAD (1980s–)
Problem | Women left out of development | Women included on unequal, exploitative terms | Unequal power relations between women and men
Solution | Integrate women: projects, credit, training | Change the global and class structures | Empower women; transform gender relations
Roots | Liberal feminism; Boserup (1970) | Marxist and dependency theory | Socialist feminism; Moser, Kabeer
Critique of NGOs | — | NGO projects "add women" without changing structures | NGOs must support women's own organising
:::

**Applying WAD to Pakistan.** Micro-credit and handicraft projects for women brought income to many households, but WAD critics note that they rarely touched **land ownership, feudal power, wage exploitation or the unpaid care** that absorbs women's time. A home-based embroiderer trained by a project still sells to a middleman at piece rates. From the WAD standpoint, this is integration into an unequal system — the very "technocratic project" the 2026 question describes.

## 10.5  Gender mainstreaming — technocracy or transformation?

**Gender mainstreaming**, endorsed at Beijing (==1995==), asks governments to assess every policy for its effects on women and men. In Pakistan it produced gender units, **Gender Reform Action Plans** (2000s), gender-responsive budgeting pilots and training. Critics say it became a **checklist exercise** run by consultants, where "gender" means a workshop rather than a shift of power. Supporters reply that mainstreaming is only as political as the people who use it.

## 10.5b  Gender mainstreaming in Pakistan's government

After Beijing, Pakistan adopted a **National Plan of Action** (==1998==) and a **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women** (==2002==). Donor-supported **Gender Reform Action Plans** in the 2000s aimed to increase women in the civil service, create gender units in ministries and introduce **gender-responsive budgeting** in some provinces. The **NCSW** was strengthened by an Act of ==2012==, and after the **18th Amendment** (==2010==) women's development became a provincial subject, with provincial commissions and departments. Supporters point to more women in government jobs and new data on women; critics describe many units as understaffed, dependent on project money, and quick to fade when donor funding ended.

## 10.5a  Voices on both sides in Pakistan

Pakistani activists themselves have debated NGO-isation. Veterans of WAF insisted from the start on staying a **voluntary, unfunded platform**, precisely to keep their independence. Researchers have described how donor money after Beijing created a class of **gender consultants** and **project staff**, and how some rural women came to see NGOs as employers rather than allies. Yet many of the same activists worked in NGOs such as Shirkat Gah and Aurat Foundation, whose research and lobbying made the laws of 2006–2016 possible. The debate is therefore not "NGOs good or bad" but **how movements and NGOs should relate**.

::: balance | The verdict to write
NGO-isation **partly** depoliticised Pakistan's women's movement: donor agendas, projectisation and professionalisation weakened mass mobilisation, as the WAD critique predicts. But NGOs also **produced laws, data and services**, and the movement has **re-politicised** itself through WAF's legacy, rural struggles and the Aurat March. The way forward is a **division of labour**: NGOs for expertise and services, independent movements for mobilisation and politics — with local funding and accountability to women themselves.
:::
